WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Phil Bauscher asked if I would be open to giving a PharmaSug keynote about our learnings in AI in late August of last year.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:17.000
Even then, I was worried about whether what we had learned about the intersection of the AI space and the needs of our customers would be relevant by the time I stood in front of all of you, 10 months later.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.000
These are the three questions we said we would address today.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:37.000
And while I will be touching on all of those items, my goal is to have you walk out of here with a deeper appreciation of how to think about the changes that are increasingly important as you decide how you can help your organization and yourself.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:42.000
As a reminder, this talk will not give you a formula or predictions.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:56.000
What it will give you is our current account of our exploration and learnings that have changed our thinking in ways we didn't expect, and the principles we have arrived at that we believe can give you a foundation for your own path forward and the decisions you will be looking to make.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:04.000
Given the speed with which the world around us is changing, I expect that some parts of this talk will become completely irrelevant in a year, or maybe sooner.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:10.000
However, this is the latest thinking we have from the conversations with dozens of customers and Posit's own experiences with AI.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:15.000
Before we dive in, I want to make sure everyone knows who we are as a company.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.000
For those of you who may be less familiar with Posit, let me give you a quick background.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:27.000
We have been building open source tools for data science and statistical computing for 17 years.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:36.000
Our tools, the Tidyverse, Shiny, Quarto, R Markdown, and of course RStudio are used by millions of data scientists and statisticians around the world.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:48.000
The pharmaceutical space, specifically, our platform is used by the majority of the world's top pharma companies for everything from drug discovery to regulatory statistical analysis and clinical reporting.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:54.000
In 2016, JJ and I learned about Public Benefit Corporations.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:00.000
We realized that this was the legal construct we had been looking for and one that articulated the type of company we wanted to build.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:14.000
So, in 2019, we completed our conversion from a C-Corp to a Public Benefit Corporation, which requires us to make a legal commitment to ensure that the interests of the scientific community, not only our shareholders, are part of our charter.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:21.000
In 2022, we re-revented the company to be Posit Software.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:27.000
Our commitment to science is central to the company's mission, and our approach is enshrined in our charter.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:34.000
It is a key tenet of how we think about the tools we build, and it all starts with the Prime Directive.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:44.000
The idea at the heart of everything we're talking about today is from a gentleman by the name of John Chambers.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:48.000
He's one of the most important figures in the history of statistical computing.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:54.000
He created the S programming language at AT&T Bell Labs, which was implemented as R in the 90s.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:03:00.000
He taught at Stanford, and in his book in 2008, he articulated what he called the Prime Directive.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:09.000
The Prime Directive is this. Software for data analysis must be trustworthy, and it must be shown to be trustworthy.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:13.000
If you read that carefully, it's not enough for your analysis to be correct.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:17.000
The correctness must be demonstrable. The computation must be transparent.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:23.000
Anyone who receives the results of your work has limited opportunity to verify the results of direct observation.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:29.000
They have no choice but to trust the analysis, and by extension, the software that produced it.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:32.000
That places an enormous obligation on all of us.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:36.000
This is why we've always believed in code-first data science.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:39.000
Code is the mechanism by which you satisfy the Prime Directive.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:45.000
Code can be read, code can be reviewed, code can be tested, reproduced, and audited.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:56.000
A low-code or point-and-clicks interface cannot make the same promise unless every operation it performs is expressible as code that others can read, review, and reproduce.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:00.000
This is the exact lens through which we evaluate every AI claim.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:06.000
Not, is it impressive, or is it fast, but can it be trusted, and can that trust be proven?

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:10.000
So why code-first?

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:17.000
The Prime Directive is not just a philosophy. In a GXP environment, it is a practical necessity.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:24.000
When a regulator asks you to explain your analysis, the code and your SOPs are your answer.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:29.000
When an auditor needs to verify that the method was applied correctly, the code is your evidence.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:35.000
When a colleague needs to reproduce your results six months later, the code is your insurance.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:41.000
This is why we have invested in making code-first data science more powerful, more accessible, and more reproducible.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:47.000
Not because it's trendy, but because it is the only architecture that makes the Prime Directive achievable in a regulated environment.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:55.000
Keep this principle in your mind as we go through the rest of the talk, because the question of whether AI can be used responsibly in your work comes down to one thing.

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:58.000
Can it be made to satisfy the Prime Directive?

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:07.000
I want to frame today's talk as a single journey seen through two lenses, in essence, two strands of the same helix that cannot be pulled apart.

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:14.000
The first strand is technical and organizational. It is about what Gen-AI can and cannot do in a regulated data science environment.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:19.000
The risks, the guardrails that need to be developed, and what we have already learned along the way.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:25.000
The second strand is human. It is about change, the very reality of change.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:32.000
It is about change, the very real anxiety of watching your field transform faster than feels comfortable.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:43.000
The question of what your expertise is worth in a world where machines can write code, and the mindset shifts that I believe are necessary to navigate this without either freezing up or throwing caution to the wind.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:54.000
I want to treat these separately at first, because conflating them too early is part of what makes this topic so hard to navigate and discuss with customers, prospects, and our own employees.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:59.000
But by the end of the morning, I think you will see why they can't ultimately be pulled apart.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:02.000
Both strands are hard. Both are necessary.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:09.000
And I have to admit that we have not mastered either one at Posit, but we have learned several lessons that we hope are applicable to you.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:17.000
Before we talk about AI, I want to take a minute to remind us all the foundation that we are standing on.

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:24.000
The tools we use every day, the infrastructure that made the first R-based FDA submissions possible, didn't appear from thin air.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:38.000
The R pilot group, Pharmaverse, the reproducible analysis workflows that are now standard in clinical reporting, were built on a foundation that the Pharma data science community, the R Consortium, and Posit built together over more than a decade.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:46.000
It was our customers who helped us understand their needs, highlighted the gaps in the software we were building, and often amazed us with the innovations they came up with.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:52.000
We have learned from many of you in this room, and we are so very grateful to be able to help with this important work.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:58.000
This collaboration, grounded in trust and community, has enabled the field to take a giant step forward.

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:03.000
The pharmaceutical company is collaborating on tools while competing on molecules.

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:07.000
This trust is what we are all trying to preserve as we leap into this new world of AI.

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:13.000
We're looking to extend the same principles we have already proven in a regulated environment.

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:19.000
Reproducibility, transparency, audibility, and code as the source of truth.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:25.000
The foundation we are standing on was built carefully, deliberately, and with the prime directive in mind.

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:28.000
That is exactly the standard we have to hold our use of AI to.

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:35.000
I thought it might be helpful to share our own evolution in thinking about the applicability of AI.

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:42.000
It is not super polished, but it captures four moments in time that can paint a picture of the arc and the speed with which change is coming.

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:53.000
In February of 2024, we hosted a company-wide work week in Minneapolis in the dead of winter because of the cost and direct flight convenience.

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:58.000
For one of our sessions, we invited 20 customers and prospects to join us for a conversation.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:05.000
As you can imagine, the question everyone wanted answered was, what does Posit think about AI and the use of AI for data science?

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:13.000
We put six of our most brilliant technical minds on a stage, our CTO, our architects, engineering leaders, and some of our most senior data scientists.

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:21.000
Each was asked, in their own area of expertise, to share their perspective on whether Gen AI could play a meaningful and safe role in data science.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:29.000
The verdict was unanimous. None could see how Gen AI could be used for serious regulated data science in any safe or meaningful way.

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:36.000
The probabilistic nature of large language models, the inability to validate outputs, and the risk of hallucination were not theoretical concerns.

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:39.000
They were architectural problems we couldn't see a way around.

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:42.000
These were not timid people hedging their bets.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:46.000
These were genuinely brilliant people giving you their frank and best thinking.

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:47.000
And they were wrong.

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:54.000
Or rather, they were right about the state of things in February 2024, and the world moved faster than any of us expected.

00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:03.000
One year later, in the same venue, we watched our CTO, Joe Cheng, unveil a small experiment he called DataBot.

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:10.000
In front of all of us, Joe downloaded a data set he had never seen before in a domain he had no personal expertise in.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:17.000
He opened DataBot, an agentic AI assistant, that could visualize data, slice and dice it, ask questions about it, and suggest next steps.

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:20.000
And he started exploring the data in front of us.

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:24.000
In under 10 minutes, he had developed a genuine feel for the data set.

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:29.000
He had identified data quality issues, found interesting patterns, and generated visualizations.

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:33.000
He had asked questions that would have taken hours of manual exploratory work to answer.

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:37.000
And he got answers he could verify by reading the code that it wrote.

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:40.000
The room was quiet.

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:44.000
This wasn't just a demo of a faster way to do something we already knew how to do.

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:47.000
It was a demonstration of something qualitatively different.

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:52.000
An AI that could serve as an active intellectual collaborator in the early stages of data science work,

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:56.000
accelerating the path from raw data to understanding.

00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:59.000
Our thinking shifted. Not all the way, mind you.

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:05.000
The concerns about validation, about regulated environments, about the prime directive were all still completely valid.

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:09.000
But the question changed. It was no longer, can this be useful?

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:14.000
It was, under what conditions can this be used responsibly?

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:19.000
Then came November of 2025.

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:24.000
We, as a technical and product team, tend to have a bit of a skeptical approach to promises of technology.

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:27.000
It is too easy to get caught up in the hype otherwise.

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:32.000
With the Cloud Sonnet 4.5 release in September, Cloud Opus 4.5 release in November,

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:38.000
along with the improvements to Cloud Code, some of our most senior engineers moved from being skeptics to advocates.

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:41.000
That exploded across the tech industry during the Christmas break,

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:46.000
when everyone woke up to what the early adopters had discovered a few weeks earlier.

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:50.000
GPT 5.2's release in December further stoked that fire.

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:54.000
It is clear that the threshold has been crossed.

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:58.000
The ability to build real applications, debug complex code, iterate on problems,

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:03.000
and have the AI not just generate a first draft, but actively collaborate through the debugging cycle,

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:08.000
was transformative in a way that is hard to describe if you haven't experienced it.

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:12.000
For those of us who had been watching and playing with these tools,

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:15.000
the question had already shifted with DataBot in February.

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:17.000
In November, this was something different.

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:22.000
It was the moment, someday this will change everything, became, today is that someday.

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:24.000
At least for software engineering.

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:27.000
The abstract promise had landed.

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:33.000
For the statisticians, statistical programmers, people whose work involves building, debugging, and validating code,

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:38.000
I'd encourage you to find a way to play with some of these tools, even if it is on your own dime.

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:42.000
We launched Posit.AI in March specifically to make that easier.

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:47.000
It includes a free trial that lets you experience the capabilities with your own personal data.

00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:51.000
In April, I joined Phil Bauscher, our industry director of life sciences,

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:55.000
on a tour of 11 pharma and life sciences companies in Japan.

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:59.000
We also met with nine additional companies when we attended the stats programming council.

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:05.000
At the council meeting, Phil demoed the capabilities of AI tooling in our latest IDE, Positron.

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:09.000
For nine of our customer visits, he ran the team through a two- to four-hour workshop

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:13.000
that provided a safe sandbox environment for them to explore and understand

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:17.000
how to use AI to help build, debug, and understand data.

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:21.000
We use Positron for the demos and workshops because of the deeper native integration

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:28.000
across programming languages since data science teams are increasingly multilingual with Python and SAS.

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:33.000
We could have used RStudio since the AI integrations for the R language are virtually identical.

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:37.000
The good news was that the reaction we saw from customers and prospects in Japan

00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:42.000
has been consistent with what our visits across the U.S. and Europe have shown,

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:44.000
whether in pharma or other industries.

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:49.000
That makes it easier to gain conviction that we are working on a problem with wide applicability.

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:53.000
Internally, the shift in our engineering to use AI for coding

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:58.000
has also sparked some genuinely interesting questions about the nature of the work itself.

00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:03.000
We've been exploring tools like RoboRev from Wes McKinney for code reviews in engineering,

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:07.000
which when paired with the latest models can have superhuman capabilities,

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:13.000
detecting correctness issues and understanding how two far-flung pieces of code can interact subtly.

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:17.000
For some of our engineers, the question now is not whether a human has reviewed the PR,

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:20.000
but whether an agent has reviewed it.

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:25.000
We have talked about giving our customers the ability to introduce adversarial agent models for data science

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:30.000
in which code and conclusions can be challenged by agents with different contexts.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:33.000
And underneath all of that is a question we keep coming back to,

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:38.000
one that I want you to hold on to because we will return to it later in the talk.

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:40.000
Who actually wrote the code?

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:44.000
I'm not going to answer that now, but I want you to notice that it's not really a technical question.

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:45.000
It is a human one.

00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:50.000
And it is the question that connects everything in strand one to everything in strand two,

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:52.000
the human side of change.

00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:59.000
Mid-2024, we were already saying we need to figure out how to do this right.

00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:04.000
What changed between then and now is that the question stopped being one that forward-looking teams were wrestling with

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:06.000
and became one that everyone is wrestling with.

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:08.000
This is not a small shift.

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:10.000
And the speed of change, the capabilities for coding,

00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:15.000
especially, have become impossible to ignore in a matter of months, not years.

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:21.000
We are all at varying speeds and with varying degrees of comfort, desperately trying to adapt.

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:25.000
The technology is not going to stabilize and wait for the field to catch up.

00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:27.000
The models will continue to improve.

00:14:27.000 --> 00:14:30.000
The capabilities will continue to expand.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:34.000
Just 12 days ago, Michelle Ryder from Sanofi gave a talk at BioIT

00:14:34.000 --> 00:14:38.000
where she estimated that by 2030, 45% of R&D activities will, quote,

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:43.000
undergo significant transformation, creating unprecedented opportunities for productivity gains

00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:45.000
and breakthrough discoveries.

00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:48.000
If I were a betting man, I would bet that it won't take that long.

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:52.000
The pressure from your organizations to engage with these tools will continue to grow.

00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:57.000
So the question is no longer whether Gen AI has a role in pharmaceutical data science.

00:14:57.000 --> 00:15:02.000
Something that can explore a data set in 10 minutes and build a working application in 20 minutes has a role.

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:07.000
The question is how do we ensure that its role satisfies the prime directive?

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:11.000
How do we ensure the work can be trusted and shown to be trusted?

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:14.000
That is what the rest of this talk is about.

00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:21.000
Over the past two years, we've had conversations with dozens of pharmaceutical data science leaders

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:23.000
across North America, Europe, and Japan.

00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:26.000
The concerns are remarkably consistent.

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:31.000
Five objections come up in almost every conversation, whether it's in pharma, healthcare,

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:33.000
or even governmental agency.

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:37.000
So let us tackle them one by one.

00:15:43.000 --> 00:15:45.000
We cannot validate AI-generated code.

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:48.000
This is the most common objection.

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:53.000
It spawns several smaller questions, including can we trust code that a machine wrote?

00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:56.000
And the answer is not without some means of confirming its correctness.

00:15:56.000 --> 00:16:00.000
There are important best practices that the industry is still learning and codifying.

00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:05.000
Still, the approach we are committed to is one where every piece of an analysis is written as code

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:08.000
and therefore is inspectable.

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:12.000
Another question is who is responsible when it is wrong?

00:16:12.000 --> 00:16:15.000
And the answer is ultimately you are.

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:19.000
You can't tell your management team that the AI said that this was the right answer.

00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:24.000
For some organizations, you have seen the practice of double programming.

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:27.000
Ultimately, the goal is to make sure that two independent individuals

00:16:27.000 --> 00:16:30.000
will get the same results given the same inputs.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:32.000
You can simulate that type of process with AI.

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:36.000
Most importantly, you need to make sure you understand the work produced

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:38.000
and ensure that it is well documented and tested.

00:16:38.000 --> 00:16:41.000
The readability and succinctness of R plus the tidyverse

00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:44.000
should be particularly helpful for organizations

00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:48.000
as a human's ability to review massive amounts of code is the next bottleneck.

00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:52.000
Can an auditor verify the process?

00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:55.000
And the answer is absolutely, because the output of the work is code

00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:58.000
that is reproducible, inspectable, and testable.

00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:02.000
There should be no reason why an auditor would have an issue with it.

00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:05.000
The fact that an AI wrote that code is irrelevant.

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:09.000
What is relevant is that the testing of the code followed your SOPs.

00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:12.000
The GXP anxiety is very real.

00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:14.000
AI systems are probabilistic.

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:17.000
They can generate code that looks correct when it may not be.

00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:18.000
They can miss edge cases.

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:20.000
They can be confidently wrong.

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:22.000
But guess what?

00:17:22.000 --> 00:17:24.000
So can humans.

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:27.000
But here's what we have learned.

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:30.000
The answer is not to keep humans out of the loop as a safety check at the end.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:33.000
The answer is to design the human into the loop

00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:36.000
as an architectural requirement throughout.

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:40.000
The statistician or programmer is not the last line of defense against AI errors.

00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:42.000
They are the pilot.

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:46.000
AI is a copilot that can do a great deal of useful work.

00:17:46.000 --> 00:17:49.000
But the pilot always has a hand on the controls,

00:17:49.000 --> 00:17:51.000
always understands what's going, what is happening,

00:17:51.000 --> 00:17:54.000
and always bears responsibility for the outcome.

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:56.000
Code First makes this possible.

00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:59.000
When AI outputs our code,

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:02.000
our readable, executable, and version-controlled code,

00:18:02.000 --> 00:18:05.000
the human expert can review, understand, test, and validate the output

00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:08.000
before it becomes part of the permanent record.

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:12.000
That is the prime directive applied to AI-assisted work.

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:18.000
The second objection is AI will replace our statisticians.

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:21.000
I mentioned our company-wide workweek in the dead of winter in Minneapolis

00:18:21.000 --> 00:18:23.000
in 2024 and 2025.

00:18:23.000 --> 00:18:25.000
This year, we held it in Monterey, California,

00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:28.000
to give us all an opportunity to see each other outdoors.

00:18:28.000 --> 00:18:31.000
See, I'm not entirely heartless.

00:18:31.000 --> 00:18:34.000
My keynote focused on the traits and mindsets that we all need

00:18:34.000 --> 00:18:37.000
in order to navigate the changes stemming from AI.

00:18:37.000 --> 00:18:40.000
Our team's fears echoed those of some of our customers

00:18:40.000 --> 00:18:45.000
who wonder what would become of them if 50, 70, or even 100% of their day-to-day work

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:48.000
was no longer relevant in this new world.

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:53.000
The truth is, none of us can really predict which jobs will disappear altogether.

00:18:53.000 --> 00:18:58.000
The introduction of cars in the early 1900s eliminated many roles

00:18:58.000 --> 00:19:01.000
and radically curtailed the number of other jobs.

00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:05.000
For today, I believe it is most helpful to focus on jobs

00:19:05.000 --> 00:19:08.000
that will continue to exist, but whose means of doing the work

00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:10.000
are fundamentally changed.

00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:13.000
Let me offer you an analogy.

00:19:13.000 --> 00:19:16.000
Here's a man in this image.

00:19:17.000 --> 00:19:22.000
And everybody has identified that this person is a carpenter.

00:19:22.000 --> 00:19:25.000
Here's another man in this image.

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:30.000
And again, they both have the same title,

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:34.000
and they both may have the same level of commitment to their craft.

00:19:34.000 --> 00:19:37.000
But if you're building a deck at your home,

00:19:37.000 --> 00:19:39.000
one of them will finish the work in three days,

00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:41.000
and one will take two weeks.

00:19:41.000 --> 00:19:43.000
Who do you hire?

00:19:43.000 --> 00:19:44.000
The answer is obvious.

00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:47.000
We hire the person with the power tools

00:19:47.000 --> 00:19:50.000
because they've respected our time and resources enough

00:19:50.000 --> 00:19:52.000
to master the best tools available.

00:19:52.000 --> 00:19:54.000
In a world of power tools,

00:19:54.000 --> 00:19:56.000
the handsaw is no longer the professional standard.

00:19:56.000 --> 00:19:59.000
That does not mean that there's no place for the handsaw

00:19:59.000 --> 00:20:01.000
and the hammer in this new world,

00:20:01.000 --> 00:20:04.000
but the bulk of the work will be handled by the power tools.

00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:07.000
The statistician and programmer who uses AI tools

00:20:07.000 --> 00:20:10.000
to accelerate their work is not a lesser statistician or programmer.

00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:12.000
They are the statisticians and programmers

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:14.000
who finish the job in three days.

00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:17.000
What AI eliminates is the syntax tasks,

00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:20.000
the hours spent on translation errors,

00:20:20.000 --> 00:20:22.000
boilerplate code, and copying and pasting.

00:20:22.000 --> 00:20:26.000
What it frees is judgment, domain expertise, and scientific insight.

00:20:26.000 --> 00:20:29.000
Those are precisely the things that took decades to develop

00:20:29.000 --> 00:20:32.000
and that no AI currently replicates.

00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:34.000
I know that some in this room are likely saying,

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:37.000
but what good is speed if we fail an audit?

00:20:37.000 --> 00:20:39.000
And that is a fair concern.

00:20:39.000 --> 00:20:43.000
Speed doesn't have to mean less attention or lower accuracy.

00:20:43.000 --> 00:20:45.000
Just as cutting a piece of wood with a power saw

00:20:45.000 --> 00:20:48.000
doesn't have to be less accurate than using a handsaw.

00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:51.000
The domain knowledge you have built over your career,

00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:53.000
how clinical trials work,

00:20:53.000 --> 00:20:55.000
what the regulatory standards require,

00:20:55.000 --> 00:20:59.000
what the science demands is most valuable in this new era, not less.

00:20:59.000 --> 00:21:01.000
The AI amplifies that knowledge.

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:03.000
It doesn't replace it.

00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:06.000
I had thought that the carpenter analogy was useful in March

00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:08.000
and apparently terrifying to some of our employees,

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:10.000
as I recently learned.

00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:13.000
A few weeks later, I realized that it is incomplete

00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:16.000
and falls into the common trap

00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:18.000
when we think about the risk with AI.

00:21:18.000 --> 00:21:21.000
And I want to show you why with an example from your own world.

00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:23.000
Think about the process of generating tables,

00:21:23.000 --> 00:21:26.000
listings, and figures at the end of a trial phase.

00:21:26.000 --> 00:21:28.000
Now ask yourself, what if the question isn't,

00:21:28.000 --> 00:21:30.000
how do I generate TLS faster?

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:32.000
But why are we waiting until the end of the trial

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:35.000
to look at safety signals at all?

00:21:35.000 --> 00:21:37.000
That question isn't about tools and speed.

00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:40.000
It's about the system and the constraints in the system.

00:21:40.000 --> 00:21:42.000
We'll come back to that question,

00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:44.000
but hold it for now because it is the question

00:21:44.000 --> 00:21:46.000
that separates those who think of themselves

00:21:46.000 --> 00:21:48.000
as carpenters with better tools

00:21:48.000 --> 00:21:50.000
and those who reimagine themselves

00:21:50.000 --> 00:21:54.000
as the person who designs and builds the whole outdoor space,

00:21:54.000 --> 00:21:57.000
the deck, the wiring, the grilling station.

00:21:59.000 --> 00:22:01.000
Objection three.

00:22:01.000 --> 00:22:04.000
We can't trust the models with our data.

00:22:05.000 --> 00:22:07.000
This is a governance question, not an AI question,

00:22:07.000 --> 00:22:09.000
and it has a governance answer.

00:22:09.000 --> 00:22:11.000
I've had at least a half a dozen conversations

00:22:11.000 --> 00:22:14.000
over the past few months about this topic.

00:22:14.000 --> 00:22:16.000
The concern is legitimate.

00:22:16.000 --> 00:22:20.000
Patient data, trial data, proprietary compound information,

00:22:20.000 --> 00:22:22.000
historically, there would be no scenario

00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:25.000
in which that data could leave your systems.

00:22:25.000 --> 00:22:28.000
For some companies, that requirement is still true,

00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:31.000
and local models may well be the right answer here

00:22:31.000 --> 00:22:35.000
if your organization is not using any cloud capabilities.

00:22:36.000 --> 00:22:39.000
The good news is that local models

00:22:39.000 --> 00:22:42.000
have had a genuine step change in performance this spring,

00:22:42.000 --> 00:22:46.000
particularly Google's Gemma 4 and Alibaba's Quen 3.5

00:22:46.000 --> 00:22:48.000
and newer models, bringing them much closer

00:22:48.000 --> 00:22:52.000
to the mainstream usefulness for this class of work.

00:22:52.000 --> 00:22:55.000
However, many more companies have already embraced the cloud

00:22:55.000 --> 00:22:58.000
or used some of the top data platforms.

00:22:58.000 --> 00:23:00.000
There's another path forward that will allow you

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:03.000
to access these models safely.

00:23:03.000 --> 00:23:06.000
While the models themselves are stateless

00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:09.000
and your prompts and data are not stored by the model,

00:23:09.000 --> 00:23:11.000
the providers all have the ability,

00:23:11.000 --> 00:23:14.000
and in almost all cases, the legal requirement

00:23:14.000 --> 00:23:16.000
to store the prompts and responses

00:23:16.000 --> 00:23:19.000
for safety or abuse reasons.

00:23:19.000 --> 00:23:21.000
There is a concept called zero data retention,

00:23:21.000 --> 00:23:23.000
known in the industry as ZDR,

00:23:23.000 --> 00:23:28.000
which is provided by a few of the cloud providers

00:23:28.000 --> 00:23:30.000
and data platforms today,

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:33.000
but it is not uniformly available across all the platforms.

00:23:33.000 --> 00:23:35.000
ZDR is meant to ensure that the provider

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:37.000
is not permanently storing the prompts

00:23:37.000 --> 00:23:39.000
in addition to the usual commitments

00:23:39.000 --> 00:23:41.000
of not using the data for any training.

00:23:41.000 --> 00:23:42.000
It would be fair to take issue

00:23:42.000 --> 00:23:44.000
with the current definition of zero

00:23:44.000 --> 00:23:46.000
since providers like Anthropic

00:23:46.000 --> 00:23:48.000
will retain enough summary and metadata information

00:23:48.000 --> 00:23:52.000
to be able to ensure user safety classifier results

00:23:52.000 --> 00:23:54.000
and enforce their usage policy,

00:23:54.000 --> 00:23:57.000
even in a platform that supports ZDR.

00:23:57.000 --> 00:23:59.000
We started out with AWS Bedrock

00:23:59.000 --> 00:24:01.000
for our access to cloud code,

00:24:01.000 --> 00:24:03.000
and we're comfortable with the level of agreements

00:24:03.000 --> 00:24:06.000
we have with AWS,

00:24:06.000 --> 00:24:07.000
even though they are retaining the prompts

00:24:07.000 --> 00:24:09.000
and responses for some time.

00:24:09.000 --> 00:24:12.000
The agreement we put together in place with Anthropic

00:24:12.000 --> 00:24:15.000
does allow us to configure ZDR for our usage,

00:24:15.000 --> 00:24:17.000
although the data retention is configurable

00:24:17.000 --> 00:24:20.000
by our IT and governance teams.

00:24:20.000 --> 00:24:22.000
IT and governance teams will push back

00:24:22.000 --> 00:24:24.000
on the use of AI because of the concerns

00:24:24.000 --> 00:24:26.000
that data is moving outside the boundaries

00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:28.000
that they have defined for it.

00:24:28.000 --> 00:24:30.000
Our counter argument is that for most

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:31.000
of the customers we have met,

00:24:31.000 --> 00:24:36.000
they are already trusting AWS, Azure, GCP,

00:24:36.000 --> 00:24:38.000
Databricks, Snowflake, and Palantir

00:24:38.000 --> 00:24:40.000
with the security of their systems and their data,

00:24:40.000 --> 00:24:43.000
and they likely already have the legal agreements

00:24:43.000 --> 00:24:45.000
that would protect them.

00:24:45.000 --> 00:24:47.000
There is no world we see where your team

00:24:47.000 --> 00:24:51.000
is better off without secure, approved access to AI tools.

00:24:51.000 --> 00:24:52.000
You will be using hand tools

00:24:52.000 --> 00:24:54.000
to compete against people with power tools.

00:24:54.000 --> 00:24:57.000
This is not a winnable game in the long term.

00:24:57.000 --> 00:24:59.000
Instead, our approach has been to build

00:24:59.000 --> 00:25:01.000
the right level of scaffolding and guardrails

00:25:01.000 --> 00:25:03.000
that organizations will need to allow

00:25:03.000 --> 00:25:06.000
their teams to truly flourish.

00:25:06.000 --> 00:25:07.000
The foundational pieces are available

00:25:07.000 --> 00:25:09.000
at the infrastructure level.

00:25:09.000 --> 00:25:10.000
We're moving through the backlog quickly

00:25:10.000 --> 00:25:12.000
to add the guardrails that our conversations

00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:15.000
with all of you have highlighted.

00:25:15.000 --> 00:25:16.000
The good news is that we are able

00:25:16.000 --> 00:25:18.000
to go faster than ever,

00:25:18.000 --> 00:25:23.000
which is both frightening and exhilarating.

00:25:23.000 --> 00:25:24.000
Objection four.

00:25:24.000 --> 00:25:27.000
We don't know which model to trust.

00:25:27.000 --> 00:25:28.000
This is a very real concern.

00:25:28.000 --> 00:25:30.000
However, the answer is not to wait

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:32.000
for an evaluation of a given model

00:25:32.000 --> 00:25:34.000
before you give your teams access to AI.

00:25:34.000 --> 00:25:36.000
The reality is that models are changing

00:25:36.000 --> 00:25:40.000
at breakneck speed and leapfrog each other regularly.

00:25:40.000 --> 00:25:43.000
The organization's focus should be on building systems

00:25:43.000 --> 00:25:46.000
that enable you to switch between models.

00:25:46.000 --> 00:25:48.000
We have some customers who can only run

00:25:48.000 --> 00:25:50.000
on Azure and use Copilot,

00:25:50.000 --> 00:25:53.000
and others may be locked into Anthropic or Gemini.

00:25:53.000 --> 00:25:55.000
We believe that organizations will need the flexibility

00:25:55.000 --> 00:25:58.000
to seamlessly switch between models,

00:25:58.000 --> 00:26:01.000
possibly even for different tasks or use cases.

00:26:01.000 --> 00:26:03.000
What was state-of-the-art in mid-November

00:26:03.000 --> 00:26:06.000
had changed by the end of the month.

00:26:06.000 --> 00:26:07.000
Like the validation process,

00:26:07.000 --> 00:26:10.000
which varies widely across organizations,

00:26:10.000 --> 00:26:13.000
our belief is that we should make it as easy as possible

00:26:13.000 --> 00:26:16.000
for you to understand how the tools are being used

00:26:16.000 --> 00:26:19.000
and to assess the quality of the work being produced.

00:26:19.000 --> 00:26:21.000
Your strategy for AI adoption shouldn't start

00:26:21.000 --> 00:26:24.000
with ensuring you have a trustworthy model.

00:26:24.000 --> 00:26:26.000
It should start with accepting

00:26:26.000 --> 00:26:30.000
that you can never trust any model implicitly.

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:32.000
The answer is to shift the validation target.

00:26:32.000 --> 00:26:34.000
What you validate is the environment,

00:26:34.000 --> 00:26:38.000
the governed system within which the output of the AI operates,

00:26:38.000 --> 00:26:41.000
the reproducible, auditable infrastructure,

00:26:41.000 --> 00:26:43.000
the human review process,

00:26:43.000 --> 00:26:45.000
the code-first output standard,

00:26:45.000 --> 00:26:47.000
the version control and traceability

00:26:47.000 --> 00:26:50.000
that make every step of the analysis inspectable.

00:26:50.000 --> 00:26:53.000
A model that operates within a well-governed environment

00:26:53.000 --> 00:26:56.000
producing code-first outputs

00:26:56.000 --> 00:26:58.000
that a qualified human reviews and approves

00:26:58.000 --> 00:27:00.000
satisfies the prime directive,

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:03.000
regardless of which underlying model generates it.

00:27:03.000 --> 00:27:05.000
The architecture is what you validate,

00:27:05.000 --> 00:27:09.000
not the weights of the model.

00:27:09.000 --> 00:27:13.000
Objection five, we are not ready yet.

00:27:13.000 --> 00:27:15.000
We are in those very same shoes.

00:27:15.000 --> 00:27:18.000
In February 2024, if you had asked us, we have said, no.

00:27:18.000 --> 00:27:21.000
And there's genuine wisdom in that instinct.

00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:23.000
The risks of moving carelessly in a regulated environment

00:27:23.000 --> 00:27:25.000
are very real.

00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:27.000
Posit's own story is instructive here.

00:27:27.000 --> 00:27:29.000
We were genuinely skeptical to start,

00:27:29.000 --> 00:27:30.000
but we started experimenting.

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:32.000
We found a responsible approach

00:27:32.000 --> 00:27:34.000
and started introducing AI into our products

00:27:34.000 --> 00:27:36.000
in a manner that we could defend

00:27:36.000 --> 00:27:38.000
and with the caveats that we thought were important.

00:27:38.000 --> 00:27:41.000
The November 2025 releases gave us the confidence

00:27:41.000 --> 00:27:44.000
to push our organization harder on the adoption curve

00:27:44.000 --> 00:27:46.000
because our regular engagement with the tools

00:27:46.000 --> 00:27:50.000
meant we could see exactly how much the models had advanced.

00:27:50.000 --> 00:27:52.000
There's a genuine discomfort that we see

00:27:52.000 --> 00:27:55.000
when talking to our customers and even our own employees,

00:27:55.000 --> 00:27:58.000
and they often fall into one of two buckets.

00:27:58.000 --> 00:28:00.000
In one bucket, the questions are,

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:02.000
how do I know that this is safe?

00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:04.000
And where are the guardrails?

00:28:04.000 --> 00:28:08.000
And in the second bucket is, what is my role in this new world?

00:28:08.000 --> 00:28:10.000
We should not confuse the discomfort

00:28:10.000 --> 00:28:12.000
of doing something differently than we have before

00:28:12.000 --> 00:28:15.000
with the worry that what we are doing is wrong.

00:28:15.000 --> 00:28:18.000
There's also a coupling of our professional identity

00:28:18.000 --> 00:28:22.000
with the tasks we have grown up mastering.

00:28:22.000 --> 00:28:24.000
Within Posit, we are still working through

00:28:24.000 --> 00:28:27.000
what this means for a variety of roles in the organization

00:28:27.000 --> 00:28:32.000
in development, QA, sales, marketing, finance, and data science.

00:28:32.000 --> 00:28:34.000
We have people who have restructured

00:28:34.000 --> 00:28:36.000
their entire workday around these tools,

00:28:36.000 --> 00:28:38.000
and others who are deeply uncertain

00:28:38.000 --> 00:28:41.000
whether that restructuring is responsible at all.

00:28:41.000 --> 00:28:43.000
Both groups have a point.

00:28:43.000 --> 00:28:45.000
Here's what I can tell you with confidence.

00:28:45.000 --> 00:28:48.000
Using AI effectively is a skill.

00:28:48.000 --> 00:28:52.000
It takes time to build, and it is genuinely hard to teach.

00:28:52.000 --> 00:28:54.000
You have to learn it through experience.

00:28:54.000 --> 00:28:57.000
Some organizations have been racing up the learning curve

00:28:57.000 --> 00:28:59.000
for well over a year,

00:28:59.000 --> 00:29:01.000
and others are still getting their shoes on.

00:29:01.000 --> 00:29:04.000
That gap is not going to close on its own.

00:29:04.000 --> 00:29:07.000
Running experiments means getting tools

00:29:07.000 --> 00:29:09.000
into the hands of a small group of people

00:29:09.000 --> 00:29:11.000
as quickly as possible.

00:29:11.000 --> 00:29:13.000
The learning only happens through doing.

00:29:13.000 --> 00:29:15.000
Organizations that spend months

00:29:15.000 --> 00:29:17.000
eliminating every conceivable risk

00:29:17.000 --> 00:29:19.000
before allowing anyone to touch AI tools

00:29:19.000 --> 00:29:21.000
are going to find themselves in trouble.

00:29:21.000 --> 00:29:23.000
There is no shortcut to learning,

00:29:23.000 --> 00:29:28.000
and frankly, learning is the new game for us humans.

00:29:33.000 --> 00:29:35.000
So here's the most concrete thing I can offer.

00:29:35.000 --> 00:29:39.000
Find one bounded problem in your current work

00:29:39.000 --> 00:29:40.000
and try something.

00:29:40.000 --> 00:29:42.000
Not because the tools are ready for everything.

00:29:42.000 --> 00:29:43.000
They are not.

00:29:43.000 --> 00:29:46.000
But because your judgment about what they are ready for

00:29:46.000 --> 00:29:47.000
will not improve

00:29:47.000 --> 00:29:50.000
until you have enough experience to have a judgment.

00:29:50.000 --> 00:29:52.000
Start narrow.

00:29:52.000 --> 00:29:53.000
Scope is the variable,

00:29:53.000 --> 00:29:55.000
not the importance of the problem.

00:29:55.000 --> 00:29:56.000
And don't wait.

00:29:56.000 --> 00:29:58.000
The risks of falling behind are too high,

00:29:58.000 --> 00:30:00.000
not just for your organization,

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:02.000
but for you personally.

00:30:06.000 --> 00:30:10.000
We promised you a list of principles.

00:30:10.000 --> 00:30:12.000
So what does responsible AI

00:30:12.000 --> 00:30:15.000
in regulated data science actually look like?

00:30:15.000 --> 00:30:17.000
Based on our own experiences,

00:30:17.000 --> 00:30:18.000
the customer conversations we've had,

00:30:18.000 --> 00:30:20.000
and the work we are doing in our products,

00:30:20.000 --> 00:30:22.000
we have arrived at four principles.

00:30:22.000 --> 00:30:24.000
They're not the final word.

00:30:24.000 --> 00:30:25.000
We are still learning,

00:30:25.000 --> 00:30:27.000
and the field is still evolving.

00:30:27.000 --> 00:30:29.000
But these are the non-negotiables

00:30:29.000 --> 00:30:31.000
as we see them today.

00:30:31.000 --> 00:30:33.000
Code first, always.

00:30:33.000 --> 00:30:36.000
AI outputs must be inspectable,

00:30:36.000 --> 00:30:40.000
reviewable, version-controlled code.

00:30:40.000 --> 00:30:43.000
Human in the loop, by design.

00:30:43.000 --> 00:30:45.000
The expert is the pilot.

00:30:45.000 --> 00:30:48.000
The AI is the co-pilot.

00:30:48.000 --> 00:30:50.000
Govern environments.

00:30:50.000 --> 00:30:52.000
Reproducible, validated environments

00:30:52.000 --> 00:30:54.000
where the system, not the individual,

00:30:54.000 --> 00:30:56.000
enforces compliance.

00:30:56.000 --> 00:30:58.000
Session aware, data private.

00:30:58.000 --> 00:31:00.000
AI that understands your context deeply

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:03.000
without your data leaving your infrastructure.

00:31:03.000 --> 00:31:05.000
We are currently working to build

00:31:05.000 --> 00:31:07.000
these principles into our tools,

00:31:07.000 --> 00:31:08.000
not as optional features,

00:31:08.000 --> 00:31:10.000
but as the default architecture.

00:31:10.000 --> 00:31:11.000
The goal is to build a system

00:31:11.000 --> 00:31:13.000
where users can, quote-unquote,

00:31:13.000 --> 00:31:15.000
fall into the pit of success,

00:31:15.000 --> 00:31:19.000
a phrase coined by Microsoft's Rico Martin in 2003

00:31:19.000 --> 00:31:22.000
and repeated by our chief scientist Hadley Wickham.

00:31:22.000 --> 00:31:24.000
We want an environment where doing the right thing

00:31:24.000 --> 00:31:26.000
is also the easy thing,

00:31:26.000 --> 00:31:28.000
where the statistician who uses AI assistance

00:31:28.000 --> 00:31:30.000
is automatically in a governed, auditable,

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:31.000
reproducible workflow,

00:31:31.000 --> 00:31:34.000
not because they remember to turn on the right settings,

00:31:34.000 --> 00:31:36.000
but because the environment made it impossible

00:31:36.000 --> 00:31:39.000
to do otherwise.

00:31:39.000 --> 00:31:41.000
Which brings us to the second strand,

00:31:41.000 --> 00:31:48.000
one that I now believe is the harder strand to solve for.

00:31:48.000 --> 00:31:49.000
Before I move into the second strand,

00:31:49.000 --> 00:31:51.000
I want to return to the question I left you with

00:31:51.000 --> 00:31:53.000
a few minutes ago.

00:31:53.000 --> 00:31:55.000
Who actually wrote the code?

00:31:55.000 --> 00:31:57.000
On the surface, it sounds like a provenance

00:31:57.000 --> 00:32:00.000
or a version control question.

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:01.000
But the more you sit with it,

00:32:01.000 --> 00:32:04.000
the more you realize it is asking something deeper.

00:32:04.000 --> 00:32:07.000
It is asking, what does authorship mean

00:32:07.000 --> 00:32:09.000
when a machine does the drafting?

00:32:09.000 --> 00:32:11.000
What does expertise mean when a tool can generate

00:32:11.000 --> 00:32:13.000
in seconds what used to take hours?

00:32:13.000 --> 00:32:15.000
What does professional identity mean

00:32:15.000 --> 00:32:17.000
when the skills you spent a decade mastering

00:32:17.000 --> 00:32:20.000
are no longer the limiting constraint?

00:32:20.000 --> 00:32:23.000
These are not questions with clean technical answers.

00:32:23.000 --> 00:32:25.000
They are human questions,

00:32:25.000 --> 00:32:28.000
and they are exactly where the two strands meet.

00:32:28.000 --> 00:32:31.000
As you likely saw through the conversation about strand one,

00:32:31.000 --> 00:32:33.000
the human dimension kept surfacing.

00:32:33.000 --> 00:32:36.000
In the carpenter analogy, in the TLF question,

00:32:36.000 --> 00:32:38.000
in the anxiety underneath objection five,

00:32:38.000 --> 00:32:40.000
these strands are intertwined

00:32:40.000 --> 00:32:42.000
because you can't really make progress on one

00:32:42.000 --> 00:32:45.000
without engaging with the other.

00:32:45.000 --> 00:32:47.000
But solving them requires radically different

00:32:47.000 --> 00:32:48.000
skill sets and approaches,

00:32:48.000 --> 00:32:50.000
which is why I wanted to keep them separate

00:32:50.000 --> 00:32:53.000
long enough to see each one clearly.

00:32:53.000 --> 00:32:57.000
The challenges of adapting to AI are not unique to Pharma.

00:32:57.000 --> 00:32:58.000
They are universal.

00:32:58.000 --> 00:33:00.000
And the mindset shifts I'm about to share

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:04.000
are the same ones we are working through at Posit every day.

00:33:06.000 --> 00:33:09.000
So what does it actually take to change?

00:33:09.000 --> 00:33:11.000
I've been thinking for a long time

00:33:11.000 --> 00:33:12.000
about what it actually takes,

00:33:12.000 --> 00:33:15.000
not to survive this transition, but to thrive in it.

00:33:15.000 --> 00:33:16.000
And I have come to believe

00:33:16.000 --> 00:33:19.000
it comes down to mindsets, not skills.

00:33:19.000 --> 00:33:20.000
Skills can be learned.

00:33:20.000 --> 00:33:21.000
Tools can be mastered.

00:33:21.000 --> 00:33:23.000
But the underlying disposition you bring

00:33:23.000 --> 00:33:25.000
to an uncertain, fast-moving environment

00:33:25.000 --> 00:33:29.000
is what determines whether you adapt or freeze.

00:33:29.000 --> 00:33:32.000
I introduced seven mindsets, I believe,

00:33:32.000 --> 00:33:35.000
that mattered at our work week in March of this year.

00:33:35.000 --> 00:33:37.000
Today, I want to focus on three

00:33:37.000 --> 00:33:39.000
that I think speak most directly

00:33:39.000 --> 00:33:40.000
to the people in this room,

00:33:40.000 --> 00:33:42.000
to what you do, who you serve,

00:33:42.000 --> 00:33:45.000
and what you will need to think through.

00:33:47.000 --> 00:33:49.000
To me, it all starts with curiosity,

00:33:49.000 --> 00:33:52.000
the refusal to accept that's just how it is.

00:33:52.000 --> 00:33:53.000
There is power in asking,

00:33:53.000 --> 00:33:55.000
why is something the way it is?

00:33:55.000 --> 00:33:57.000
Not to be disruptive for its own sake,

00:33:57.000 --> 00:33:59.000
but because the answer often reveals

00:33:59.000 --> 00:34:02.000
the assumptions that are built into the system

00:34:02.000 --> 00:34:05.000
and the constraints that no longer need to exist.

00:34:05.000 --> 00:34:06.000
I planted a question earlier

00:34:06.000 --> 00:34:07.000
and I said we'd come back to it.

00:34:07.000 --> 00:34:08.000
Here it is.

00:34:08.000 --> 00:34:11.000
Why are we waiting until the end of the trial

00:34:11.000 --> 00:34:13.000
to look at safety signals at all?

00:34:13.000 --> 00:34:15.000
Think about what the question is really asking.

00:34:15.000 --> 00:34:17.000
The process of generating tables, listings, and figures

00:34:17.000 --> 00:34:20.000
at the end of a trial phase exists for reasons.

00:34:20.000 --> 00:34:22.000
It was designed around constraints,

00:34:22.000 --> 00:34:24.000
computational, organizational, logistical,

00:34:24.000 --> 00:34:27.000
that were real at the time.

00:34:27.000 --> 00:34:29.000
But what happens when those constraints change?

00:34:29.000 --> 00:34:31.000
Some companies are no longer asking,

00:34:31.000 --> 00:34:33.000
how do I generate those TLS faster?

00:34:33.000 --> 00:34:35.000
They're asking, what becomes possible

00:34:35.000 --> 00:34:38.000
when our teams are entirely freed

00:34:38.000 --> 00:34:40.000
from writing boilerplate code?

00:34:40.000 --> 00:34:42.000
And the answer they are arriving at

00:34:42.000 --> 00:34:43.000
looks something like this,

00:34:43.000 --> 00:34:45.000
a safety monitoring board

00:34:45.000 --> 00:34:47.000
that doesn't wait for a quarterly report.

00:34:47.000 --> 00:34:50.000
Instead, they log into a secure, interactive Shiny

00:34:50.000 --> 00:34:52.000
with CVars or Teal application,

00:34:52.000 --> 00:34:55.000
where clinical data is processed end-to-end automatically,

00:34:55.000 --> 00:34:59.000
from raw data to a publication-ready Kaplan-Meier plot,

00:34:59.000 --> 00:35:02.000
allowing them to slice and dice adverse events on the fly

00:35:02.000 --> 00:35:04.000
with the underlying reproducible R code

00:35:04.000 --> 00:35:06.000
generated in the background.

00:35:06.000 --> 00:35:08.000
And it all starts with a single question

00:35:08.000 --> 00:35:10.000
asked by someone with enough curiosity

00:35:10.000 --> 00:35:14.000
to look at a workflow they had run 100 times and ask,

00:35:14.000 --> 00:35:16.000
why does this exist?

00:35:16.000 --> 00:35:17.000
And what becomes possible

00:35:17.000 --> 00:35:20.000
if it doesn't have to exist this way?

00:35:20.000 --> 00:35:23.000
That is curiosity applied at the systems level,

00:35:23.000 --> 00:35:25.000
not how do I do this faster,

00:35:25.000 --> 00:35:29.000
but what does this system exist to accomplish?

00:35:29.295 --> 00:35:35.735
But, what does this system exist to accomplish, and what becomes possible when its constraints

00:35:35.735 --> 00:35:36.735
are changed?

00:35:36.735 --> 00:35:42.575
I recently came across a thought-provoking book, Reshuffle, by Sangeet Paul Chaudhary.

00:35:38.659 --> 00:35:47.859
I recently came across a thought-provoking book, Reshuffle, by Sangeet Paul Chaudhary,

00:35:47.859 --> 00:35:52.739
in which he defines the idea of unbundling and re-bundling of work as systems are disrupted

00:35:52.739 --> 00:35:58.019
by technology. AI accelerates the breaking up of the system into its components, allowing

00:35:58.019 --> 00:36:03.099
us to restructure the work and assemble it into something new. Most of us spend our careers

00:36:03.099 --> 00:36:08.259
getting better at working inside a system. That is how we build up our expertise. But

00:36:08.259 --> 00:36:17.258
curiosity is what will allow us to step back and see the system itself.

00:36:17.258 --> 00:36:21.539
Curiosity opens the aperture, but it is customer-centricity which gives it direction. I want to offer

00:36:21.539 --> 00:36:26.139
you a reframe of what a customer means in your work, because I think most of us define

00:36:26.139 --> 00:36:32.399
it too narrowly, and that narrow definition limits what we can see. The patient is your

00:36:32.399 --> 00:36:35.958
ultimate customer. They are putting their faith into the science behind the solutions

00:36:35.958 --> 00:36:41.519
you put in the market. That is not in question. The integrity of your analysis, the reproducibility

00:36:41.519 --> 00:36:45.479
of your methods, and the trustworthiness of your submissions, all of it traces back to

00:36:45.479 --> 00:36:49.519
the people who participated in the trials, the data they generated, and the obligation

00:36:49.519 --> 00:36:55.798
to honor that contribution with science done carefully and correctly.

00:36:55.798 --> 00:37:01.339
But the patient sits inside a system. And the system has other constituents whose needs

00:37:01.339 --> 00:37:07.498
also shape the work. The regulator whose job is to ensure the science behind an approval

00:37:07.498 --> 00:37:12.339
is sound. The physician who will prescribe based on the evidence you have helped generate.

00:37:12.339 --> 00:37:17.139
The organization that has hired you, whose competitive position in its ecosystem is shaped,

00:37:17.139 --> 00:37:21.298
in part, by whether it can move faster, more reliably, and more transparently than its

00:37:21.298 --> 00:37:26.099
competitors. Here's why this matters. When you map all of these customers, not just the

00:37:26.099 --> 00:37:30.858
patient at the center, but every ring of the system surrounding them, you start to see

00:37:30.858 --> 00:37:35.618
your work differently. The question, why are we waiting until the end of the trial

00:37:35.618 --> 00:37:41.538
to look at safety signals, is not just a patient safety question. It's an organizational competitiveness

00:37:41.538 --> 00:37:46.059
question. The company that answers it first is improving its competitive position in the

00:37:46.059 --> 00:37:51.778
competitive market. But here's the uncomfortable implication. The tasks that define your role

00:37:51.778 --> 00:37:57.179
today were designed to serve all of those customers within a set of constraints. As

00:37:57.179 --> 00:38:03.578
AI removes these constraints, or reshuffles them, the tasks will change. The workflow

00:38:03.578 --> 00:38:08.539
will be unbundled and re-bundled. The question is not whether that will happen. The question

00:38:08.539 --> 00:38:13.379
is whether you will be one of the people shaping what that re-bundled version looks like, or

00:38:13.379 --> 00:38:18.019
whether someone else will shape it for you. Mapping all of your customers is how you stay

00:38:18.019 --> 00:38:22.298
oriented as the process unfolds. It is how you move from being a carpenter with better

00:38:22.298 --> 00:38:30.979
tools to someone who designs the build and builds the whole outdoor space. Curiosity

00:38:30.979 --> 00:38:34.819
opens the aperture and customer centricity gives it direction. Courage is what closes

00:38:34.819 --> 00:38:39.459
the gap between seeing and doing. Once you can see the system clearly and you have mapped

00:38:39.459 --> 00:38:43.739
and understood the customers you are serving, you'll be able to see the gaps. Seeing the

00:38:43.739 --> 00:38:48.819
gap is the first step. Acting on it is critical. We all know that the culture of pharmaceutical

00:38:48.819 --> 00:38:55.138
data science appropriately rewards caution. Errors have serious consequences. The instinct

00:38:55.138 --> 00:38:59.058
to wait until something is proven, to not put your name on something you cannot fully

00:38:59.058 --> 00:39:03.858
defend, is not timidity. It is professional discipline. It is part of what makes this

00:39:03.858 --> 00:39:09.459
feel trustworthy. But the same instinct applied without judgment to this moment becomes a

00:39:09.459 --> 00:39:15.179
different kind of risk. So let me offer you another reframe. Courage in this context is

00:39:15.259 --> 00:39:21.179
not a disposition. It is a decision. It is the decision to run one small bounded experiment.

00:39:22.459 --> 00:39:26.058
And I want to be clear about what I mean by bounded because it may not be what you expect

00:39:26.058 --> 00:39:30.459
it to be. I'm not asking you to start with unimportant work. The whole argument we've

00:39:30.459 --> 00:39:35.899
been making this morning is that AI-assisted code is inspectable, reviewable, and verifiable.

00:39:37.099 --> 00:39:42.379
The prime directive is satisfiable. If that is true, and we believe it is, then there's

00:39:42.379 --> 00:39:47.739
no reason in principle why those tools cannot be used for serious work. The risk is not in the

00:39:47.739 --> 00:39:52.459
importance of the analysis. The risk is in the scope of change you introduce before you have

00:39:52.459 --> 00:39:58.618
built the judgment to manage it well. So start narrow, pick one bounded problem, not because

00:39:58.618 --> 00:40:03.338
the tools aren't ready for serious work, but because your judgment about how to deploy them

00:40:03.338 --> 00:40:08.859
well needs room to develop. Scope is the variable, not the importance of the problem.

00:40:09.659 --> 00:40:16.139
That is the unit of courage I'm asking for. One bounded experiment on real work. And then,

00:40:16.139 --> 00:40:20.058
because the tools will have changed, the courage to run it again three months later,

00:40:20.058 --> 00:40:25.898
particularly if the first attempt didn't succeed. That willingness to rerun the experiments is a

00:40:25.898 --> 00:40:30.778
form of intellectual curiosity, an acknowledgement that what you concluded about those tools in

00:40:30.778 --> 00:40:36.299
January may no longer be true in July. That kind of willingness to revisit your own assumptions

00:40:36.299 --> 00:40:40.139
gives you an advantage over those who will write off these tools after a single

00:40:40.139 --> 00:40:44.938
disappointing experiment. We as humans struggle to think in exponential terms.

00:40:45.498 --> 00:40:49.898
What feels like a modest improvement today can look like a transformation six months from now,

00:40:49.898 --> 00:40:54.938
given tools that are growing exponentially. The experiment you set up in January needs to be

00:40:54.938 --> 00:41:00.379
revisited in July, because the tools will not be the same tools. Here's what I can tell you

00:41:00.379 --> 00:41:04.778
with confidence. The people who are running those experiments today are building something that

00:41:04.778 --> 00:41:10.218
cannot be transferred. It is not knowledge you can read in a paper or learn in a workshop.

00:41:10.218 --> 00:41:14.938
It is judgment, the accumulated sense of what these tools can and cannot do,

00:41:14.938 --> 00:41:19.979
where they are reliable, where they need to be, where they need the most careful human oversight.

00:41:19.979 --> 00:41:24.139
They are also building the muscles to try again as the models evolve. And that muscle,

00:41:24.139 --> 00:41:29.099
like judgment, can only be developed through experience. The longer it takes you to start,

00:41:29.099 --> 00:41:33.738
the wider the gap becomes between you and those who are already experimenting,

00:41:33.738 --> 00:41:39.099
learning, and refining their instincts. So find one bounded problem in your current work.

00:41:39.099 --> 00:41:43.018
Try something. Try it again in six months if you couldn't get it to work the first time.

00:41:43.018 --> 00:41:47.338
Not because the tools are perfect. They are not. But because the alternative is arriving late

00:41:47.338 --> 00:41:50.218
to a conversation that is already moving without you.

00:41:55.018 --> 00:41:59.819
You are statisticians and clinical programmers in the pharmaceutical industry. The work you do

00:41:59.898 --> 00:42:04.539
is consequential, and the standards your community develops for how AI is used in this work

00:42:04.539 --> 00:42:08.299
will matter, not just to your organizations, but to the field as a whole.

00:42:08.859 --> 00:42:12.618
The pharma-verse didn't happen because one company decided to open source their clinical

00:42:12.618 --> 00:42:17.738
reporting tools. It happened because a community of people decided that science advances faster

00:42:17.738 --> 00:42:22.218
when you build on each other's work and compete on the molecule. That instinct,

00:42:22.218 --> 00:42:26.218
the instinct that built the foundation we have been talking about all morning,

00:42:26.218 --> 00:42:33.099
is exactly what is needed now. My ask is simple. Don't wait. Start with a bounded problem.

00:42:33.738 --> 00:42:37.819
Bring the same intellectual rigor you bring to your analysis. Keep the prime directive in front

00:42:37.819 --> 00:42:42.778
of you and share what you learn. The people in this room are the right people to figure this out.

00:42:42.778 --> 00:42:46.859
You understand the science. You understand the regulatory environment. You understand

00:42:46.859 --> 00:42:51.579
what trustworthy analysis requires. The question is whether you will engage with these tools on

00:42:51.579 --> 00:42:57.898
your own terms, thoughtfully, rigorously, with your expertise intact, or whether you are willing

00:42:57.898 --> 00:43:01.819
to let someone else define your role in the re-bundled system.

00:43:05.579 --> 00:43:10.539
We aren't chasing the AI trend. We were skeptical of it in 2024. We remain skeptical of claims that

00:43:10.539 --> 00:43:14.618
don't meet the prime directive. We have no interest in selling you tools that erode the

00:43:14.618 --> 00:43:20.139
trustworthiness of your work. We believe these tools can be used responsibly because code remains

00:43:20.139 --> 00:43:24.139
at the center of the workflow, and code is precisely what the prime directive demands.

00:43:24.938 --> 00:43:29.659
Our goal has always been to serve as a neutral player. Internally, we refer to ourselves as

00:43:29.659 --> 00:43:34.859
Switzerland. We're not your cloud provider. We're not competing for your AI budget. We believe that

00:43:34.859 --> 00:43:38.778
open source tools are essential for science, and we want to make sure the tools you use

00:43:38.778 --> 00:43:43.338
are trustworthy. We've been building trustworthy tools for 17 years because the prime directive

00:43:43.338 --> 00:43:53.579
demanded it. That hasn't changed. Our commitment's to you. We will work with the community in this

00:43:53.579 --> 00:43:58.938
room, drawing on your expertise, your rigor, and your commitment to science done right,

00:43:58.938 --> 00:44:01.259
to build tools that reflect those values.

00:44:04.859 --> 00:44:09.659
First is data science, taste, and ergonomics. We will focus on tools and capabilities designed

00:44:09.659 --> 00:44:14.299
for how data science actually works, not how a generic agent thinks it should,

00:44:14.939 --> 00:44:18.378
how to keep the human in the loop while calibrating for autonomy.

00:44:22.059 --> 00:44:25.818
We need frameworks that are grounded in trustworthy and reproducible work.

00:44:25.818 --> 00:44:29.979
We want to ensure that all your work is grounded in inspectable code throughout,

00:44:29.979 --> 00:44:34.539
and governed access to upstream data sources and proper governance for deployed applications.

00:44:35.499 --> 00:44:40.059
Portability and configurability. We will ensure that you can use our tools with your favorite

00:44:40.059 --> 00:44:44.939
cloud and data platforms. What you build on these tools should be able to run anywhere.

00:44:44.939 --> 00:44:50.218
We also want to make sure that you can fine-tune and add your layers of inspectability and your

00:44:50.218 --> 00:44:57.258
own guardrails based on your own business needs. As John Chambers wrote, exploration is our mission.

00:44:57.818 --> 00:45:03.099
We and those who use our software want to find new paths to understanding the world around us.

00:45:04.059 --> 00:45:09.659
To understand the data and the underlying processes. The mission, indeed, to boldly go

00:45:09.659 --> 00:45:16.138
where no one has gone before. That mission has not changed. The tools and the mindsets have.

00:45:16.859 --> 00:45:21.499
We at Posit are investing this right. We will keep talking with you, learning from you,

00:45:21.499 --> 00:45:26.539
and building with the people in rooms like this one. If you have a story, a concern, an insight,

00:45:26.539 --> 00:45:30.378
or a challenge, we want to hear it. Thank you for your time this morning,

00:45:30.378 --> 00:45:34.138
